
#import "../../_lib/kodama.typ": *

#show: kodama

#metadata((
  title: "Nakai--Moishezon criterion",
))

Let $k$ be a field and $X$ be a proper scheme over $k$ with line bundle $scr(L)$. The Nakai--Moishezon criterion states that $scr(L)$ is ample if and only if for every closed subscheme $Y subset X$ of dimension $d$, the self-intersection number $(scr(L)^d dot Y) > 0$. It also admits an Arakelov geometrical analogue, see [TODO].

#proof()[
  We put induction on $dim X$.
  - We may assume that $X$ is integral. A line bundle $scr(L)$ is ample on $X$ if and only if its restriction to every induced reduced irreducible component is ample. In particular, $scr(L)|_Z$ is ample on every closed subscheme $Z subset.neq X$.
  - $scr(L)^(times.o n)$ is effective for some $n > 0$, so we may assume $scr(L) = scr(O)(D)$ for some effective Cartier divisor $D$. Let $scr(I) := scr(L)^(times.o -1) inter scr(O)_X$ and $scr(J) := scr(I) scr(L)$ be two non-zero quasi-coherent ideal with equality $scr(J) = scr(I) times.o scr(L)$. We have short exact sequences:
    $
      0 -> scr(I) times.o scr(L)^(times.o n) -> scr(L)^(times.o n) -> (scr(O) slash scr(I)) times.o scr(L)^(times.o n) -> 0 \
      0 -> scr(J) times.o scr(L)^(times.o n) -> scr(L)^(times.o n) -> (scr(O) slash scr(J)) times.o scr(L)^(times.o n) -> 0 \
    $
    when $n >> 0, q >= 1$, the cohomology groups vanish due to induction hypothesis:
    $
      H^(q)(X, (scr(O) slash scr(I)) times.o scr(L)^(times.o n)) = H^(q)(X, (scr(O) slash scr(J)) times.o scr(L)^(times.o n)) = 0
    $
    Hence when $q >= 2$, the cohomology groups stablize as $n >> 0$:
    $
      H^(q)(X, scr(L)^(times.o n)) = H^(q)(X, scr(I) times.o scr(L)^(times.o n)) = H^(q)(X, scr(J) times.o scr(L)^(times.o n-1)) = H^(q)(X, scr(L)^(times.o n-1))
    $
    $chi(scr(L)^(times.o n))$ tends to infinity, so $h^0(X, scr(L)^(times.o n)) > 0$ for $n >> 0$ via:
    $
      chi(scr(L)^(times.o n)) = h^0(X, scr(L)^(times.o n)) - h^1(X, scr(L)^(times.o n)) + sum_(q >= 2) (-1)^q h^(q)(X, scr(L)^(times.o n))
    $
  - $scr(O)(n D)$ is generated by global sections for some $n > 0$, so we may assume $scr(O)(D)$ is generated by global sections. Consider the short exact sequence:
    $
      0 -> scr(O)((n-1)D) -> scr(O)(n D) -> scr(O)(n D)|_D -> 0
    $
    by induction hypothesis, $H^1(X, scr(O)(n D)|_D) = 0$ as $n >> 0$ whence $
      H^1(X, scr(O)((n-1)D)) -> H^1(X, scr(O)(n D))
    $ 
    is surjective and stablizes. Hence we obtain an surjection for $n >> 0$:
    $
      H^0(X, scr(O)(n D)) -> H^0(X, scr(O)(n D)|_D)
    $
    since $scr(O)(n D)|_D$ is generated by global sections for $n >> 0$, $scr(O)(n D)$ is also generated by global sections for $n >> 0$.
  - We conclude that $f : X -> PP_k^N$ defined by $scr(O)(D)$ is finite. Due to Zariski's main theorem, every proper quasi-finite morphism is finite, so we only need to show $f$ is quasi-finite. Otherwise, there exists an integral curve $C subset X$ which is mapped to a point, contradicts to $(scr(L) dot C) > 0$.
]

